{"kind":"report","text":"Exact-head review of 0da7e483b6c33d9302885316ff7eba705c9d23b3, resting on all 30 artifacts, packages/git/README.md first. Changes requested.\n\nArchitecture: the exact cat-file type check fixes f7d273e1's non-commit head defect, with blob/tree coverage before a first publication and on an existing log. Incremental transfer fixes redundant complete-tree submission: only objects absent from the exact lease's tree are sent, the new commit is always included, and a reopened publisher trusts reconstructed object IDs only when its files rebuild that exact tree. Read-back controls promotion of present/retained state; retries keep the same parent, commit and transfer. The six local real-L/real-Git tests and dated live results support those fixes. The remaining limit cannot always be solved by the new smaller-cohort error.\n\nP2 — support a segment blob larger than one RPC transfer (packages/log/src/publisher.ts:176–184; entries.ts:82; packages/git/src/publisher/log-push.ts). The active segment is one Git blob containing up to 1,000 entries. Each next publication rewrites that whole blob, even when only one entry is new. Incremental object selection therefore does not bound its accumulated size. Once that single required blob plus the minimal transfer exceeds 64 MiB, no smaller cohort can publish the next entry; waiting or restarting cannot reach the segment rollover.\n\nIndependent DEFAULT-limit reproduction: real evaluateNotify expanded a role:member rule to a simulated directory of 2,400 distinct member IDs, each shorter than 64 characters. The signed note envelopes are tiny; the notification decisions and recipient list enlarge sealed system entries. The test uses the real LogPublisher, policy evaluator, signatures/checkpoints and MemoryGit, with no maxTransfer override. Six preliminary publications and two subsequent publications fit. At 441 entries, before the 1,000-entry segment rollover, the next transfer was 67,303,323 bytes against 67,108,864. Publishing the note alone left exactly one notified entry to publish; it still failed cohort-too-large, including after LogPublisher.open, and the ref stayed at 04669d8ecd089c41f150f671cbcea27b6857b5f2. verifyLog passed the published prefix, through 439 across ten commits. This proves the L/policy/default transfer mechanism with a simulated role directory; it is not a deployed Room/membership/Artifacts load test. Diagnostic: /tmp/artroom-checker-pushlog-v2-diagnostics/checker-default-segment.test.ts; results: /tmp/artroom-checker-pushlog-v2-default-segment.json. An additional 8,000-byte-limit case using only checkpoint entries demonstrated the same mechanism and restart result.\n\nStage bounded object uploads, including chunks of an oversized blob, before the final commit/ref publication, with owned cohort identity, type/SHA verification, retry/restart recovery and the existing exact lease fence. The final published commit must remain the one commitFor computed. Do not make a smaller admitted audience, hidden lifetime/segment ceiling, a larger fixed cap or merely a smaller Room batch the functional solution. Splitting a segment format would require an explicit protocol/layout amendment; current R-LOG-9 fixes 1,000-line segments. Include an accumulated active segment over one transfer, a one-entry next cohort, restart and independent verification in acceptance coverage. A must distinguish truly splittable new batches from a minimum object that cannot fit, and never replace a possibly applied pending cohort with different bytes.\n\nSecurity: the corrected type check and exact parent/connectivity checks run before push; the gateway still permits only the leased log update. Unknown outcomes remain unresolved, and present is promoted only after confirmation. Request bounds are useful for resource control, but should bound transport pieces rather than prohibit valid derived system entries. Read-type fallback still needs exact object SHA checks. The new live result records tree-head refusal, three exact log commits, all token revocations and a passing L verifier; it is transport evidence, not full production Room integration.\n\nSimplification: preserve one log serialization, one computed commit and one final publication fence. A bounded staging interface lets the current log layout and independent verifier continue to work without a second publication algorithm. Also keep whole-history serialization/memory costs visible in the full scalability review: each publication still owns/builds the complete log tree even when it transfers only new objects. The Node simulation does not prove the Worker's memory budget.\n\nValidation: clean root strict typecheck; 132 Git Node tests, eight Git workerd tests, 105 log Node tests, 100 log workerd tests and six real-L/real-Git tests passed. Both independent segment-limit diagnostics passed, including the default-limit case's independent prefix verification. New live driver syntax passed. All 30 candidate artifacts were read, using prior B/L reviews for unchanged blobs; all new and changed deltas and both new dated results were read fully. The isolated worktree is clean after saving diagnostics outside it. No live driver, deployment, token operation, network Git push or remote cleanup was run by checker. A's five adapter changes and actual Room end-to-end coverage remain complete scope.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e6ffbe489a15e2f71f58855837133c314cb01f91","binding":"assigned","head":"0da7e483b6c33d9302885316ff7eba705c9d23b3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:11e59c71c440e575595403797c19e25cbd7ebeac\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b84fbd41f42360fc1fc5dea2c976410e7497a150\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1eb447115d7604e520ce1fcf97d436ac79fca158","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes